\layout Subsection*

Statistical Multiplexing vs Time and Frequency division
\layout Standard

Example 3.9 in Bertsekas & Gallager
\layout Standard

Assume we have m statistically independent Poisson streams.
 Each has arrival rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\lambda }{m}$
\end_inset 

.
 Each has an average transmission time 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

If we merge the Poisson streams together, through statisical multiplexing,
 property of Poisson from Lecture 3&4 tell us that they become a Poisson
 stream with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda $
\end_inset 

.
 Average delay, from last lecture will be
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\frac{1}{\mu -\lambda }.\]

\end_inset 

Now consider an alternative approach of providing each channel with a seperate
 circuit.
 This involves splitting our transmission line into 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 channels.
 Each will now have bandwidth at most 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\mu }{m}$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

Delay in this system will now be
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\frac{1}{\frac{\mu }{m}-\frac{\lambda }{m}}=\frac{m}{\mu -\lambda }\]

\end_inset 

or 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 times larger than statistical multiplexing.
 
\layout Standard

Why does this happen?
\layout Standard

Consider the situtation where there is only one packet in the system.
 In this case much of the bandwidth will be redundant.
