\layout Standard

Let us assume the queue can contain at maximum 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 arrivals.
 The balance equation from above still applies.
 However, now the normalisation is different, giving a different value for
 
\begin_inset Formula $P(0)$
\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum _{k=0}^{m}P(0)\rho ^{k}=1\]

\end_inset 

 Since this is simply a finite sum of a geometric series 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P(0)=\frac{1}{\sum _{k=0}^{m}\rho ^{k}}=\frac{1-\rho }{1-\rho ^{m+1}}\]

\end_inset 

 We therefore have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P(k)=\frac{\rho ^{k}(1-\rho )}{1-\rho ^{m+1}}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Given a finite length 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 queue, what is the probability that an arrival is blocked from joining
 the queue (and therefore lost).
 
\layout Standard

This is answered by determining the probability of the queue being full,
 which means 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P(m)=\frac{\rho ^{m}(1-\rho )}{1-\rho ^{m+1}}\]

\end_inset 


