\layout Subsection*

Detailed Balance 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\lambda _{k-1}P\left(k-1\right) & = & \mu _{k}P\left(k\right)\\
\lambda P\left(k-1\right) & = & k\mu P\left(k\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

thus we can write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda }{k\mu }P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

and therefore
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=P\left(0\right)\left(\frac{\lambda }{\mu }\right)^{k}\frac{1}{k!}\]

\end_inset 

using the normalisation conditions we can see that
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\sum _{k=0}^{\infty }P\left(k\right) & = & 1\\
P\left(0\right) & = & \left[\sum _{k=0}^{\infty }\left(\frac{\lambda }{\mu }\right)^{k}\frac{1}{k!}\right]^{-1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

Define offered load, 
\begin_inset Formula $a=\frac{\lambda }{\mu }$
\end_inset 

, as arrival rate divided by service rate.
 This is a dimensionless constant, but has international units of Erlangs.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\left[\sum _{k=0}^{\infty }\frac{a^{k}}{k!}\right]^{-1}\]

\end_inset 

recalling that 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp \left(x\right)=\sum _{n=0}^{\infty }\frac{x^{n}}{n!}$
\end_inset 

 we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\exp \left(-a\right)\]

\end_inset 

and the steady state distribution
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{a^{k}}{k!}\exp \left(-a\right)\]

\end_inset 

which is recognised as a poisson distribution with parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

.
