\layout Subsection*

Detailed Balance Equations
\layout Standard

For a system having state less than 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $k\leq m$
\end_inset 

 the detailed balance equation is 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda P(k-1)=k\mu P(k)\]

\end_inset 

therefore for 
\begin_inset Formula $k=0\dots m$
\end_inset 

 we may write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda }{k\mu }P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

we define
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho =\frac{\lambda }{m\mu }\]

\end_inset 

and therefore
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{\lambda }{k\mu }=\frac{m\rho }{k}\]

\end_inset 

therefore we may write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{m\rho }{k}P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

and as before
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\left(m\rho \right)^{k}}{k!}P\left(0\right) &  & \textrm{for }k\leq m\label{eq:probabilities}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

For systems with state greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $k>m$
\end_inset 

 the detailed balance equation is
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda P(k-1)=m\mu P(k)\]

\end_inset 

therefore for 
\begin_inset Formula $k=m+1\dots \infty $
\end_inset 

 we may write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda }{m\mu }P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

and therefore
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\rho P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

leading to 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\rho ^{k-m}P\left(m\right)\]

\end_inset 

and substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 in (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:probabilities}

\end_inset 

) we can obtain
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(m\right)=\frac{\left(m\rho \right)^{m}}{m!}P\left(0\right)\]

\end_inset 

therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k\right)=\frac{m^{m}\rho ^{k}}{m!}P\left(0\right) &  & \textrm{for }k>m
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

thus we can write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P(k)=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
 P(0)\frac{(m\rho )^{k}}{k!}, & k\leq m\\
 P(0)\frac{m^{m}\rho ^{k}}{m!} & k>m\end{array}
\right.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Once again we can compute 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset 

 through normalisation
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\sum _{k=0}^{\infty }P\left(k\right) & = & 1\\
P\left(0\right)\left[\sum _{k=0}^{m-1}\frac{\left(m\rho \right)^{k}}{k!}+\sum _{k=m}^{\infty }\frac{m^{m}\rho ^{k}}{m!}\right] & = & 1\\
P\left(0\right) & = & \left[\sum _{k=0}^{m-1}\frac{\left(m\rho \right)^{k}}{k!}+\sum _{k=m}^{\infty }\frac{m^{m}\rho ^{k}}{m!}\right]^{-1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

consider the second term inside the bracket
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum _{k=m}^{\infty }\frac{m^{m}}{m!}\rho ^{k}=\frac{m^{m}\rho ^{m}}{m!}\sum _{k=m}^{\infty }\rho ^{k-m}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

substitute in 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{\prime }=k-m$
\end_inset 

 and we obtain
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{m^{m}\rho ^{m}}{m!}\sum _{k^{\prime }=0}^{\infty }\rho ^{k^{\prime }}=\frac{m^{m}\rho ^{m}}{m!}\frac{1}{1-\rho }\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

if 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho <1$
\end_inset 

.
 Thus we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\left[\sum _{k=0}^{m-1}\frac{\left(m\rho \right)^{k}}{k!}+\frac{m^{m}\rho ^{m}}{m!\left(1-\rho \right)}\right]^{-1}\]

\end_inset 

one quantity of interest is the probability of queueing, 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{Q}$
\end_inset 

.
 What is the probability, when the system is at steady state, that a customer
 will arrive and find all the 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 servers busy? 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\geq m\right)=\sum _{k=m}^{\infty }P\left(k\right)=P\left(0\right)\sum _{k=m}^{\infty }\frac{m^{m}\rho ^{k}}{m!}=P\left(0\right)\frac{\left(\rho m\right)^{m}}{m!}\sum _{k=m}^{\infty }\rho ^{k-m}\define C\left(m,a\right)\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

making the same substitution as above this can be re-written
\begin_inset Formula \[
C\left(m,a\right)=P\left(0\right)\frac{\left(\rho m\right)^{m}}{m!\left(1-\rho \right)}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

This is known as the 
\emph on 
Erlang C formula
\emph default 
, or Erlang's second formula after A.K.
 Erlang a pioneer of queueing theory.
 By defining, 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

, the offered load to be
\begin_inset Formula \[
a=\frac{\lambda }{\mu }=m\rho \]

\end_inset 

 and substituting into the above we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
C(m,a)=\frac{P(0)a^{m}}{m!\left(1-\frac{a}{m}\right)}.\]

\end_inset 

Nowadays we can compute the probability of waiting on a computer, however
 for the purposes of exams and exercises we will use the old fashioned method
 of curves.
